## Why This Is Asked
Tests 128-bit rotation across word boundary without per-bit loops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit value as two 64-bit words; cross-word mixing; edge k<64 vs k>=64

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi, lo; } uint128_t;
static inline uint128_t rotl128(uint128_t x, unsigned k){
  k %= 128; uint128_t r;
  if (k < 64){
    r.hi = (x.hi << k) | (x.lo >> (64 - k));
    r.lo = (x.lo << k) | (x.hi >> (64 - k));
  } else { unsigned kk = k - 64;
    r.hi = (x.lo << kk) | (x.hi >> (64 - kk));
    r.lo = (x.hi << kk) | (x.lo >> (64 - kk));
  }
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test boundary cases k=0, 63, 64, 127?
- Could you adapt to 256-bit using 4x uint64_t without loops?

## Why This Is Asked

Explores a new angle: 128-bit popcount via per-half 64-bit parallel counting using a fixed, loopless sequence of masks.

## Key Concepts

- Bit-twiddling for popcount
- Handling 128-bit values by splitting into 64-bit halves
- No branches or loops; purely arithmetic masks

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline uint32_t popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

uint32_t popcount128(uint128 x) {
  return popcount64(x.lo) + popcount64(x.hi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for 256-bit using the same pattern? 
- What are the trade-offs of inlining vs. a dedicated intrinsic on your compiler/architecture?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft branchless bit-twiddling in 128-bit space, a common low-level optimization task.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit integer handling via two 64-bit halves
- Trailing-zero count (ctz) tricks without branches
- De Bruijn sequences for fast bit indexing

## Code Example
```c
unsigned countTrailingZeros128(uint128 x) {
  static const unsigned char deBruijn64[64] = {
    0, 1, 2, 53, 3, 7, 54, 27,
    4, 38, 8, 34, 55, 48, 28, 20,
    5, 39, 53, 6, 59, 29, 42, 16,
    9, 35, 60, 50, 45, 27, 21, 17,
    10, 49, 56, 40, 30, 14, 43, 15,
    11, 23, 51, 52, 41, 19, 13, 44,
    12, 26, 58, 36, 31, 37, 29, 22
  };
  unsigned loIdx = ((x.lo & -x.lo) * 0x03f79d71b4cb0a89ull) >> 58;
  unsigned hiIdx = ((x.hi & -x.hi) * 0x03f79d71b4cb0a89ull) >> 58;
  unsigned tzLo = deBruijn64[loIdx];
  unsigned tzHi = deBruijn64[hiIdx];
  unsigned isLoZero = (x.lo == 0);
  return (1 - isLoZero) * tzLo + isLoZero * (64 + tzHi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to support a variable-width 128-bit type with non-power-of-two lanes?
- Compare performance of this approach against a masked binary search approach on modern CPUs.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of 128-bit arithmetic using hi/lo, avoiding loops, and applying a scalable, branchless popcount.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit representation via hi/lo
- SWAR/parallel bit counting
- Endianness and safe casting between types

## Code Example
```c
static inline uint64_t popcount64(uint64_t x){
  x -= (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

uint8_t popcount128(uint128 x){
  return (uint8_t)(popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo));
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to accumulate using wider accumulators? 
- What is the performance impact on platforms with native popcount intrinsics?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests compact, deterministic bit-trickery without loops, showing knowledge of parallel popcount progressions.

## Key Concepts
- Masks for pairwise sums
- Shifts to propagate counts
- Edge-case: 0 yields 0

## Code Example
```javascript
uint32_t popcount32(uint32_t x) {
  x = (x & 0x55555555) + ((x >> 1) & 0x55555555);
  x = (x & 0x33333333) + ((x >> 2) & 0x33333333);
  x = (x & 0x0F0F0F0F) + ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F);
  x = (x & 0x00FF00FF) + ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF);
  x = (x & 0x0000FFFF) + ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF);
  return x & 0x3F;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for 64-bit inputs without 128-bit math?
- How to test exhaustively across 32-bit space?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests applying a simple bitwise identity (Gray code) across a non-native 128-bit representation, including cross-boundary shifts. It enforces branch-free, constant-time thinking and careful hi/lo handling.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit arithmetic using 64-bit halves
- Gray code relation: G = X ^ (X >> 1)
- Cross-boundary shifts between hi and lo

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint128 toGray128(uint128 x){
  uint64_t hiGray = x.hi ^ (x.hi >> 1);
  uint64_t loGray = x.lo ^ ((x.lo >> 1) | (x.hi << 63));
  return (uint128){.hi = hiGray, .lo = loGray};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify correctness with randomized tests without branches?
- What’s the throughput difference when implemented on 128-bit wide arithmetic vs emulated two 64-bit halves?

## Why This Is Asked
Real-world bitfield extraction often needs to pull a contiguous block from a 128-bit value into the least-significant bits for parsing, with no branching for performance.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit arithmetic using two 64-bit halves
- Bit masking and shifting across a 128-bit boundary
- Pure bitwise manipulations (no loops)

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 extractBitBlock128(uint128 x, unsigned start, unsigned width) {
  // Build mask in 128-bit form: width ones at LSBs
  uint128 M = {0,0};
  if (width == 128) { M.hi = ~0ULL; M.lo = ~0ULL; }
  else if (width < 64) { M.hi = 0; M.lo = (width==0)?0:((1ULL<<width)-1); }
  else { M.lo = ~0ULL; M.hi = (width-64==0)?0:((1ULL<<(width-64))-1); }
  // Shift right by start and apply mask
  // Implement 128-bit shift and AND here respecting the architecture
  uint128 y = {0,0};
  // (Assume helper shifts across hi/lo are available)
  y = shrl128(x, start);
  return and128(y, M);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize for width exactly 64 or 128 in terms of branchless masks?
- How would you adapt to architectures lacking native 128-bit types?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to decompose a 128-bit operation into smaller parallel units and efficiently apply a well-known branchless counting technique.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) popcount for 32-bit lanes
- 128-bit value decomposition into hi/lo components
- Branchless bit manipulation and final reduction

## Code Example
```c
unsigned int popcount32(unsigned int x) {
    x = x - ((x>>1)&0x55555555);
    x = (x&0x33333333)+((x>>2)&0x33333333);
    x = (x+(x>>4))&0x0F0F0F0F;
    return (x*0x01010101)>>24;
}

unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x) {
    return popcount32(x.lo) + popcount32(x.lo >> 32) + 
           popcount32(x.hi) + popcount32(x.hi >> 32);
}
```

## Quick Tests
```c
assert(popcount128(0) == 0);
assert(popcount128(UINT128_MAX) == 128);
assert(popcount128(0xF0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0) == 32);
```

Why this is asked
- Tests ability to implement 128-bit rotation without branching, using a safe 128-bit intermediate. Covers edge cases n=0 and n=64 cleanly.

Key concepts
- 128-bit arithmetic, circular shift, branch-free implementation, handling wrap-around.

Code example
```c
uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned n){
  __uint128_t full = ((__uint128_t)x.hi << 64) | x.lo;
  unsigned k = n & 127;
  __uint128_t r = (full << k) | (full >> (128 - k));
  uint128 out; out.hi = (uint64_t)(r >> 64); out.lo = (uint64_t)r; return out;
}
```

Follow-up questions
- How would you implement rotateRight128 without 128-bit type?
- Discuss performance when 128-bit shifts are not optimized by the compiler.

## Why This Is Asked
This checks understanding of fixed-sequence bit tricks and performance constraints in bitfield work.

## Key Concepts
- Hamming weight via parallel summation
- Masks like 0x5555555555555555, 0x3333333333333333, 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F

## Code Example
```javascript
// Implementation using BigInt for 64-bit precision
function popcount64(x) {
  x = (x) & 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFn;
  x = x - ((x >> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333n) + ((x >> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n);
  x = (x + (x >> 4n)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn;
  x = x + (x >> 8n);
  x = x + (x >> 16n);
  x = x + (x >> 32n);
  return Number(x & 0x7Fn);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- What are the trade-offs vs lookup-table popcount in fixed-width code?
- How would you adapt this for SIMD or wider widths?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests cross-word bit ops and branchless parity reduction. It exercises combining two 64-bit halves and a compact reduction, scalable to wider architectures.

## Key Concepts

- Parity via XOR folding
- 128-bit composition hi^lo
- Branchless reductions

## Code Example

```c
int parity128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  uint64_t x = hi ^ lo;
  x ^= x >> 32;
  x ^= x >> 16;
  x ^= x >> 8;
  x ^= x >> 4;
  return x & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to a 256-bit value?  - Can you adapt this for streaming parity on large data chunks?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests cross-boundary bit manipulation and 128-bit arithmetic, which are fundamental skills in cryptography and high-performance kernel development. It also evaluates the ability to implement rotation operations without branching logic.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit rotation across two 64-bit limbs
- Using a single 128-bit intermediate simplifies boundary handling
- Edge-case handling via rotation count masking (eliminates branches)

## Code Example

```cpp
void rotl128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, unsigned int k, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  __uint128_t v = ((__uint128_t)hi << 64) | lo;
  k &= 127;
  __uint128_t r = (v << k) | (v >> (128 - k));
  *out_hi = (uint64_t)(r >> 64);
  *out_lo = (uint64_t)r;
}
```

## Edge Cases

- `k = 0`: Returns original value unchanged
- `k = 64`: Swaps hi and lo parts completely

## Why This Is Asked
Tests constant-time, multi-word popcount without branches. Bridges knowledge of SWAR patterns from 64-bit into 128-bit by handling halves separately. It also checks handling of non-power-of-two widths.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR/popcount on 64-bit
- 128-bit word handling (hi/lo)
- Branch-free, mask-shift arithmetic

## Code Example
```javascript
uint32_t countSetBits64(uint64_t v){
  v = v - ((v>>1)&0x5555555555555555ULL);
  v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v>>2)&0x3333333333333333ULL);
  v = (v + (v>>4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (v * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

uint32_t countSetBits128(uint128 x){
  return countSetBits64(x.lo) + countSetBits64(x.hi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you generalize to 256-bit values?
- How to verify edge cases (all-ones, zero) across both halves?

## Why This Is Asked
Branchless 128-bit comparison is common in crypto/hash pipelines and requires careful borrow logic across halves. It tests low-level reasoning without relying on 128-bit native ops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit numbers split into hi/lo.
- Borrow propagation across 64-bit halves.
- Branchless evaluation using comparisons on unsigned integers.

## Code Example
```c
uint8_t lessThan128(uint128 a, uint128 b){
  uint64_t loDiff = a.lo - b.lo;
  uint64_t borrowLo = (a.lo < b.lo);
  uint64_t hiDiff = a.hi - b.hi - borrowLo;
  uint64_t borrowHi = (a.hi < b.hi + borrowLo);
  return (uint8_t)borrowHi;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to extend to 128-bit equality without extra branches?
- How would you adapt for signed 128-bit comparison?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to derive circular adjacency via rotation, and to implement a 128-bit popcount using SWAR style reductions; ensures understanding of cross-boundary bit relationships.

## Key Concepts

- Circular adjacency using ROTL
- 128-bit representation as two 64-bit halves
- SWAR/popcount with bitwise masks and shifts

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline uint128 rotl128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r;
  r.hi = (x.hi << 1) | (x.lo >> 63);
  r.lo = (x.lo << 1) | (x.hi >> 63);
  return r;
}

static inline unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x){
  uint64_t a = x.hi;
  uint64_t b = x.lo;
  a = a - ((a >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  a = (a & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((a >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  a = (a + (a >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  unsigned int ca = (a * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;

  b = b - ((b >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  b = (b & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((b >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  b = (b + (b >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  unsigned int cb = (b * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;

  return ca + cb;
}
```

Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to non-power-of-two widths?
- What are performance implications on vectorized lanes?

